# Studio Class Booking — environment variables
#
# Copy to `.env` for local development:   cp .env.example .env
# Never commit `.env` — it is covered by .gitignore.
# Real credentials live in the host's secret store (Vercel / GitHub Actions), never here.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database (required)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PostgreSQL connection string used by the application at runtime.
# Local development (matches docker-compose.yml):
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://studio:studio@localhost:5432/studio_dev?schema=public"

# In production this is the POOLED endpoint, e.g.:
#   DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:pass@host/db?sslmode=verify-full"
# TLS: the app enforces VERIFIED TLS in code for any non-local host
# (rejectUnauthorized: true — see src/lib/db.ts resolvePoolConfig), so a remote
# server whose certificate does not verify is REFUSED, and any `sslmode` in this
# URL is stripped before use. That means `sslmode=no-verify` / `sslmode=disable`
# cannot silently downgrade production — they are neutralised, including when the
# host arrives via a `host=` query param. Prefer `sslmode=verify-full` here anyway
# so the migration CLI (below), which does read the URL's sslmode, verifies too.
# Intentionally unsupported (so they cannot weaken TLS): client-certificate auth
# via sslcert/sslkey, and verify-ca-without-hostname — use DATABASE_CA_CERT for a
# private root instead.
# Note: `pgbouncer=true` / `connection_limit` URL params are dead letters with
# the Prisma 7 driver adapter — node-postgres ignores them. Pool size is set in
# code and tuned by DATABASE_POOL_MAX (below).

# Optional: max size of the runtime connection pool (default 10). Behind a
# Supabase-class TRANSACTION POOLER on a multi-instance/serverless deploy, pin a
# small per-instance ceiling so N instances cannot storm Postgres, e.g.:
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX="3"

# Optional: PEM contents of a private CA that signs the database's certificate,
# for providers whose root is not in the public trust store. Supplied as an
# environment value (never committed). When set, this CA becomes the SOLE trust
# anchor for the connection (Node's `ca` option REPLACES the system store, it
# does not append) — so set it only when the DB cert chains to this private root,
# not when it chains to a public root. When unset, the system CA store is used.
# DATABASE_CA_CERT="-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\n...\n-----END CERTIFICATE-----"

# Integration tests only: overrides which database the test harness targets.
# The harness DROPs and remigrates the schema it points at, refuses any
# database whose name does not end in "test", and defaults to studio_test on
# the DATABASE_URL server when unset. CI sets it explicitly.
# TEST_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://studio:studio@localhost:5432/studio_test"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not yet consumed by the application — added in the phase that needs them.
# Listed here so the full inventory is visible in one place.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Schema & migrations: the DIRECT, non-pooled endpoint (prisma migrate reads it
# via prisma.config.ts, preferring it over DATABASE_URL). Migrations cannot run
# through a transaction pooler. The migration CLI honours this URL's sslmode
# directly, so verify TLS here explicitly:
# DIRECT_URL="postgresql://user:pass@host/db?sslmode=verify-full"

# Phase 3 (authentication): NO secret required — a deliberate reversal of the
# original plan (see docs/decisions.md #13). Sessions are opaque 32-byte
# random tokens stored hashed in Postgres; nothing needs signing.

# IANA timezone the studio operates in. Every "today" the booking logic (and
# the later alert window) computes derives from it. Optional; defaults to UTC.
STUDIO_TIMEZONE="Europe/London"

# Phase 7 (seed data): guard on the seed script. Never set in production.
# ALLOW_SEED="false"
